WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.470
Imagine walking into a review with a dashboard redesign.

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:08.450
You have evidence that people struggle with the current screen.

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:10.250
You have a proposed fix.

00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:13.130
You put up the first slide and someone asks,

00:00:13.130 --> 00:00:16.350
“Is this the best use of engineering time right now?”

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:18.100
That is a reasonable question.

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:22.280
It may feel like a rejection, but it is also a clue.

00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:24.800
The person across the table is being asked to

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:26.920
trade one piece of work for another.

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:29.350
A pile of research about the screen does not

00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:30.890
yet explain the trade.

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:33.300
Chapter Four of Accelerated tells this as the

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:34.970
dashboard that almost died.

00:00:34.990 --> 00:00:37.360
The first pitch described the confusing

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:39.470
dashboard and asked for a redesign.

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:41.920
The later pitch put options on the table,

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:44.700
including a smaller change, a larger bet, and a

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:47.390
delay. The book reports a decision after the

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:50.290
frame changed. In this workshop we use the case

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:52.860
to study the question, not to promise the same

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:55.490
result. What would an owner need to believe

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:57.400
before moving engineering time?

00:00:57.420 --> 00:00:59.080
That is where discovery starts.

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:02.140
Open the priority page you made in Lesson Two.

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:04.620
You chose one path and wrote an assumption that

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:07.090
could overturn it. Now write the decision

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:09.080
owner’s question in their words.

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:11.470
For the dashboard, it might be, “What are we

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:14.130
giving up to do this, and what would improve if

00:01:14.150 --> 00:01:15.190
we did?” For our

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:17.870
fictional membership service, it might be,

00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:20.750
“Will a guided first task help people begin, or

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:22.960
will it add another step?” That is more

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:25.390
useful than “Do users like the idea?” The

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:26.310
answer you believe

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:28.290
today goes on the next line.

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:31.100
Label it provisional. Under that, write the

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:33.800
strongest objection a thoughtful colleague

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:37.020
could raise. The objection is not a nuisance to

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:38.440
remove from the slide.

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:39.370
It tells you what

00:01:39.910 --> 00:01:41.220
evidence the decision needs.

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:43.820
The chapter argues that insight can land flat

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:46.090
when its frame ignores the risk the other

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:48.460
person carries. So we work backward:

00:01:48.860 --> 00:01:52.310
decision, likely answer, objection, evidence.

00:01:52.390 --> 00:01:54.800
Only then do we choose what to observe or ask.

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:57.450
The order keeps research attached to action.

00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:01.300
A single polished idea invites a yes or no.

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:04.700
Chapter Four calls this the binary trap.

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:09.420
The dashboard case becomes easier to discuss

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:11.700
when the team can compare a quick simplification,

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:14.240
a deeper rebuild, and a deliberate delay.

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:16.680
Each path has a different cost and a different

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:17.950
thing it could teach.

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:19.820
Your priority file already has two

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:22.090
alternatives. Bring them forward rather

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:24.240
than hiding them after you choose.

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:26.830
For the membership example, the welcome email

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:28.370
could be the light change.

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:30.820
The guided task is the small behavior test.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:32.210
The staff dashboard is a

00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:34.820
visibility investment. None of those labels is

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:35.310
a fact about

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:37.860
customers. They are ways to make the trade

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:40.850
visible. If the objection is “We are solving

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:43.180
the wrong part of onboarding,” the evidence

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:45.650
should tell you where a person stops and why.

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:47.450
If the objection is “We cannot support

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:49.950
another workflow,” the evidence may need to

00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:52.650
include staff effort. A stronger frame does not

00:02:52.670 --> 00:02:55.030
mean louder language. It means the choice is

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:56.250
clear enough for another

00:02:56.750 --> 00:02:58.760
person to challenge. Suppose you can ask only

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:00.980
one more question before the review.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:02.700
What would change your mind?

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:04.830
That question keeps discovery honest.

00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:05.310
If you would

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:07.990
make the same recommendation no matter what the

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:11.030
answer is, you may be collecting support for a

00:03:11.050 --> 00:03:12.760
story rather than testing it.

00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:13.500
The book suggests

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:16.460
pressure testing a pitch from different angles

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:17.440
and anticipating

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:19.820
objections. You can do that with a safe,

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:22.060
fictional brief before anyone sees the

00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:24.750
recommendation. Ask what a skeptical finance

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:26.980
lead, an operator, or a customer might

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:29.300
question. Then compare those possible

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:31.400
objections with what real evidence is

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:34.110
available. An AI tool can help you rehearse the

00:03:34.130 --> 00:03:35.310
questions. It cannot

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:37.810
turn a simulated objection into a customer

00:03:37.830 --> 00:03:41.040
interview. On our card, write one evidence gap

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:43.350
that matters. Maybe we do not know whether

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:45.800
people abandon the first step because they

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:47.450
cannot find it, cannot understand

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:49.110
it, or do not value it.

00:03:49.130 --> 00:03:50.930
Those are different problems.

00:03:50.950 --> 00:03:53.390
We should not build a guided path for all three

00:03:53.410 --> 00:03:55.860
without learning which one we have.

00:03:56.070 --> 00:03:57.860
Let's fill the rest of the card.

00:03:57.970 --> 00:04:00.540
The decision is whether to test a guided first

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:03.490
task. Our current answer is yes, because the

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:06.260
team suspects people need a clearer starting

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:06.830
point.

00:04:07.329 --> 00:04:09.826
The strongest objection is that the first step

00:04:09.846 --> 00:04:12.079
may already be clear; maybe the real issue

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:12.677
comes later.

00:04:13.643 --> 00:04:15.757
The missing evidence is an observation of where

00:04:15.777 --> 00:04:18.189
people hesitate and a short question about what

00:04:18.209 --> 00:04:19.503
they expected to happen next.

00:04:19.877 --> 00:04:22.323
A safe test could use a prototype and willing

00:04:22.351 --> 00:04:24.337
participants under an approved research

00:04:24.357 --> 00:04:24.670
process.

00:04:25.253 --> 00:04:27.571
We would record what they did, what they said,

00:04:27.813 --> 00:04:29.107
and what remains uncertain.

00:04:29.477 --> 00:04:30.729
That last field matters.

00:04:31.117 --> 00:04:33.094
A few observations can direct the next

00:04:33.122 --> 00:04:33.392
question;

00:04:33.890 --> 00:04:36.336
they do not establish a population wide result.

00:04:36.748 --> 00:04:38.598
If you have no approved research access,

00:04:39.181 --> 00:04:40.347
keep the card as a plan.

00:04:40.913 --> 00:04:43.146
Do not manufacture quotes or silently

00:04:43.217 --> 00:04:45.536
substitute simulated people for participants.

00:04:46.118 --> 00:04:48.360
The dashboard case in the book helps us see how

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:50.784
a named trade can make evidence relevant.

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:53.666
Our exercise turns that lesson into an action

00:04:53.822 --> 00:04:55.800
you can take without pretending that the answer

00:04:55.870 --> 00:04:56.610
is already known.

00:04:57.022 --> 00:04:58.488
Pause and make your own card.

00:04:58.857 --> 00:05:00.237
Write the decision in a sentence.

00:05:00.479 --> 00:05:02.157
Write the answer you currently believe.

00:05:02.846 --> 00:05:04.525
Write the strongest fair objection.

00:05:05.150 --> 00:05:07.477
Then write one piece of evidence that could

00:05:07.497 --> 00:05:10.072
change the decision and a safe way to seek it.

00:05:10.228 --> 00:05:12.546
If your evidence is already available, record

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:13.528
its source and date.

00:05:13.769 --> 00:05:15.277
If it is missing, say missing.

00:05:15.785 --> 00:05:18.146
Your file should now show a line from priority

00:05:18.174 --> 00:05:19.469
choice to evidence gap.

00:05:19.838 --> 00:05:21.696
That line is the point of these first three

00:05:21.716 --> 00:05:24.170
lessons. A good question can save you from a

00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:26.040
month of defending the wrong artifact.

00:05:26.595 --> 00:05:27.293
When you resume,

00:05:27.705 --> 00:05:29.383
look at the person behind the evidence.

00:05:30.009 --> 00:05:32.242
We tend to summarize that person as a persona

00:05:32.355 --> 00:05:34.631
and leave the slide untouched for a year.

00:05:35.111 --> 00:05:37.003
In the next lesson we will make the customer

00:05:37.031 --> 00:05:39.819
situation more specific and the persona easier

00:05:39.975 --> 00:05:40.501
to revise.

00:05:41.214 --> 00:05:43.370
You do not have to win the room with a perfect

00:05:43.390 --> 00:05:46.250
story today. You need a decision that another

00:05:46.270 --> 00:05:49.030
person can understand, an objection you have

00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:51.640
taken seriously, and evidence you would

00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:53.030
actually listen to.

00:05:53.535 --> 00:05:55.512
Keep the card attached to your priority page.

00:05:55.967 --> 00:05:57.048
We are going to use it again.
